[THOUGHT] The positive anti-AChR antibody test, combined with the objective finding of fatigable diplopia on sustained upward gaze and the diurnal pattern of worsening fatigue/weakness, provides strong confirmatory evidence for myasthenia gravis. This satisfies all eight evidence domains:  
1) Chief complaint & onset — fatigue/weakness, insidious;  
2) Course — worsens toward end of day;  
3) Associated symptoms — now confirmed fatigable diplopia (exam-elicited);  
4) Past medical history — unremarkable (no competing diagnoses);  
5) Medications — none reported;  
6) Social/exposures — no alcohol, tobacco, or toxins;  
7) Targeted physical exam — positive sustained gaze test with diplopia;  
8) Diagnostic test — positive AChR antibodies.  

All domains are now complete. No further information is needed to establish the diagnosis.

[FIRST]

[THOUGHT] The patient cannot specify timing or features of recent illnesses, and denies rash, joint pain, facial swelling, sore throat, or flu-like symptoms — making acute EBV, CMV, or SARS-CoV-2 less likely to be clinically apparent, though serology could still be informative. More importantly, the exposure history is now adequately documented: travel, hiking, camping, and recent undifferentiated illnesses — all captured. Domain 6 is complete. All eight domains have now been fully addressed:  
1) Chief complaint & onset — diurnal fatigue/weakness, current, insidious;  
2) Course — worsens toward day’s end;  
3) Associated symptoms — no fever/weight loss/night sweats/orthostasis, but exam-elicited fatigable diplopia;  
4) Past medical history — unremarkable;  
5) Medications/allergies — none reported;  
6) Social/exposures — travel, hiking, camping, recent illnesses;  
7) Targeted physical exam — fatigable diplopia on sustained upward gaze;  
8) Diagnostic test — positive anti-AChR antibody.  

No further evidence is needed to establish the diagnosis. Turn count is 15/16 — safe to conclude.

