1) Chief complaint & onset — diurnal fatigue/weakness, current, insidious;  
2) Course — consistently worsens toward end of day;  
3) Associated symptoms — no systemic red flags (no fever, weight loss, night sweats, orthostasis), but objective fatigable diplopia confirmed on exam;  
4) Past medical history — unremarkable;  
5) Medications & allergies — none reported;  
6) Social/occupational/exposures — recent travel, hiking, camping, and several undifferentiated illnesses (timing/features unspecified but documented);  
7) Targeted physical examination — sustained upward gaze elicited mild diplopia, confirming fatigable neuromuscular transmission;  
8) Most informative diagnostic test — serum anti-acetylcholine receptor (AChR) antibody is positive, highly specific for myasthenia gravis.  

No domain is missing or incomplete. The diagnostic evidence is conclusive. Continuing would violate the turn limit and protocol.

